(function(){
  window.VN_LEARNING=window.VN_LEARNING||{};
  const M=(id,title,highYield,teaching,keyPoints,visual,mnemonic,goodToKnow,practiceConnection,activity)=>({id,title,highYield,teaching,keyPoints,visual,mnemonic,goodToKnow,practiceConnection,activity});
  const V=(title,kind,items)=>({title,kind,items});
  const N=(title,cue,explanation)=>({title,cue,explanation});
  const A=(prompt,options)=>({prompt,options});
  const O=(text,correct,feedback)=>({text,correct,feedback});
  window.VN_LEARNING['lesson-1']={
    version:2,title:'Vegetarianism',chapter:'Foundations + current evidence',duration:'95–115 minutes',
    outcomes:[
      'Distinguish vegan, lacto, ovo, lacto-ovo and flexible plant-forward patterns without treating identity labels as nutrient assessments.',
      'Separate ethical, environmental, cultural and health motivations from claims that require scientific testing.',
      'Recognize why raw, fruitarian and highly restrictive patterns carry distinct nutrition risks.',
      'Define macrobiotic eating as a variable philosophy-shaped pattern, evaluate its nutrient risks and reject unsupported disease-cure claims.',
      'Explain major food-system environmental pathways without relying on a single dramatic ratio.',
      'Use respectful, non-assumptive questions when supporting a vegetarian client.'
    ],
    modules:[
      {id:'language',title:'Define the pattern before evaluating it',summary:'Use precise dietary-pattern language and avoid label-based assumptions.',moments:[
        M('vn1-m1','Vegetarian is an umbrella term','A dietary label tells you what is usually excluded; it does not reveal what is actually eaten or whether the diet is adequate.',[
          'Vegetarian patterns exclude flesh foods, but the foods retained vary. Lacto-ovo vegetarians include dairy and eggs; lacto vegetarians include dairy but not eggs; ovo vegetarians include eggs but not dairy; vegans avoid animal-derived foods as a dietary practice. Some people use near-vegetarian, flexitarian or plant-forward for patterns that still include occasional animal foods.',
          'The first assessment task is therefore descriptive rather than judgmental: ask what foods are included, excluded, fortified or supplemented. Two people who both say “vegan” can have very different intakes of legumes, whole grains, vegetables, calcium-fortified beverages, vitamin B12 and highly processed foods.'
        ],['Lacto does not imply eggs.','Vegan and vegetarian are not nutritionally interchangeable labels.','Actual intake matters more than identity alone.'],
        V('Pattern map','taxonomy',[{label:'Lacto-ovo',detail:'Dairy + eggs; no meat, poultry or fish.'},{label:'Lacto',detail:'Dairy; no eggs or flesh foods.'},{label:'Ovo',detail:'Eggs; no dairy or flesh foods.'},{label:'Vegan',detail:'No animal-derived foods in the dietary pattern.'}]),
        N('Decode the label','L = lacto = milk · O = ovo = egg', 'Read each prefix as an included food category, then verify what the person actually eats.'),
        'Food labels and personal identities do not always match textbook categories. Ask, rather than correcting a person’s chosen identity.',
        'Open an assessment with “Walk me through what you usually include” instead of assuming nutrient adequacy or deficiency from the label.',
        A('Which statement best supports an accurate assessment?',[O('A vegan label automatically proves the diet is nutrient dense.',false,'A label does not show pattern quality or reliable nutrient sources.'),O('Ask which foods, fortified products and supplements are actually used.',true,'This converts an identity label into assessable intake information.'),O('All vegetarians eat eggs and dairy.',false,'Only some vegetarian patterns include those foods.')])
        ),
        M('vn1-m2','Restriction changes the risk profile','The more foods a pattern removes, the more intentionally it must replace energy, nutrients and food functions.',[
          'Macrobiotic, raw-food and fruitarian diets are not simply synonyms for vegan eating. They can impose additional restrictions on food groups, cooking methods, fat sources or geographic origin. A varied vegan pattern can be well planned, while a fruit-dominant or highly raw pattern may be low in energy density, protein, vitamin B12, vitamin D, calcium, iodine, iron, zinc and essential fats.',
          'Risk also depends on life stage, appetite, food access and supplement use. Infants, children, pregnant people, older adults and anyone with low energy intake have less room for a pattern that displaces nutrient-dense foods. The safe response is not alarmism; it is a structured check for reliable nutrient sources and red flags.'
        ],['Raw or fruitarian is more restrictive than vegan.','Reliable vitamin B12 is essential in vegan patterns.','Energy density becomes critical when appetite or stomach capacity is limited.'],
        V('Restriction ladder','risk',[{label:'Plant-forward',detail:'More plant foods; few categorical exclusions.'},{label:'Vegetarian',detail:'Flesh foods excluded; other choices vary.'},{label:'Vegan',detail:'All animal-derived foods excluded.'},{label:'Raw/fruitarian',detail:'Additional restrictions can sharply narrow nutrient options.'}]),
        N('Check what disappeared','REMOVE → REPLACE', 'For every removed food function, identify an adequate replacement rather than merely an excluded item.'),
        'Fermented foods, seaweed and unfortified nutritional yeast are not dependable vitamin B12 sources.',
        'Escalate to qualified medical or dietetic support for growth faltering, neurologic symptoms, pregnancy complications, severe restriction or suspected deficiency.',
        A('Which pattern generally needs the most careful energy-density assessment?',[O('A varied lacto-ovo pattern',false,'It still needs assessment, but it retains several energy-dense food categories.'),O('A highly restrictive raw fruitarian pattern',true,'Multiple exclusions can make adequate energy and nutrient intake difficult.'),O('A plant-forward pattern with no exclusions',false,'Its range of options is broader.')])
        ),
        M('vn1-m9','Macrobiotic is a philosophy-shaped pattern—not a synonym for vegan','Macrobiotic diets usually centre whole grains, vegetables, legumes and fermented foods, but their rules and animal-food inclusion vary widely.',[
          'Modern macrobiotic practice grew from a broader philosophy of living in balance and was later organized into dietary systems. A typical version emphasizes whole cereal grains, seasonal vegetables, beans, soups, fermented soy foods and sometimes sea vegetables; some versions include fish or other animal foods. Other versions are far more restrictive. The label therefore describes a family of practices, not one reproducible nutrient prescription.',
          'Some components—whole grains, legumes and vegetables—can support fibre and micronutrient intake. Those benefits do not validate claims about balancing “yin and yang,” nor do they make the pattern a treatment for cancer or other disease. The U.S. National Cancer Institute states that there is no proof a macrobiotic diet can slow, cure or prevent cancer from returning.'
        ],['Macrobiotic patterns vary and may not be vegan.','Whole plant foods can be nutritious without proving the philosophy’s disease claims.','A restrictive version must be assessed from the actual foods and amounts eaten.'],
        V('Macrobiotic pattern map','spectrum',[{label:'Common foundation',detail:'Whole grains, vegetables, beans, soups and fermented foods.'},{label:'Variable foods',detail:'Fruit, nuts, seeds, oils, sea vegetables and fish may be limited or included depending on the version.'},{label:'Belief layer',detail:'Philosophical rules are distinct from evidence-based nutrient assessment.'},{label:'Clinical boundary',detail:'Not a substitute for cancer or medical treatment.'}]),
        N('Separate food from claim','PATTERN · PHILOSOPHY · PROOF', 'Describe the foods, identify the belief or rule, then ask what clinical evidence actually supports the claim.'),
        'NCI describes macrobiotic diets as high in unprocessed carbohydrate and centred on whole grains and vegetables, while emphasizing that disease-treatment claims are unproven.',
        'Ask which version is being followed, which foods are restricted, whether fish is included, how much oil or energy-dense food is allowed, and whether the person is using the pattern in place of prescribed care.',
        A('Which statement accurately describes a macrobiotic diet?',[O('It is one standardized vegan menu.',false,'Versions differ and some include fish or other animal foods.'),O('It is a variable, philosophy-shaped pattern often centred on whole grains and vegetables.',true,'This captures both its common foundation and its variability.'),O('It is a proven cancer treatment.',false,'NCI states that this claim is not supported.')])
        ),
        M('vn1-m10','A macrobiotic safety audit follows the restriction—not the reputation','The stricter the macrobiotic rules, the more carefully energy, protein and key micronutrients must be verified.',[
          'A grain-and-vegetable-heavy pattern may become too low in energy density, protein variety or fat when legumes, soy foods, nuts, seeds and oils are tightly limited. Depending on the foods retained, vulnerable nutrients can include vitamin B12, vitamin D, calcium, iron, zinc, iodine and omega-3 fats. Sea vegetables do not solve B12 needs, and their iodine content can be highly variable; “natural” does not mean dose-controlled.',
          'Growth, pregnancy, lactation, older age, low appetite, illness and weight restoration leave less room for restrictive rules. Red flags include unintended weight loss, fatigue, menstrual change, slowed growth, numbness or tingling, bone concerns and a refusal to use reliable fortified foods or supplements. The counselling goal is not to dismiss the person’s philosophy; it is to liberalize the pattern enough to meet needs and coordinate care when symptoms or disease are present.'
        ],['Check total energy and protein-food frequency before chasing minor details.','Verify labelled B12 and other nutrient sources; do not count seaweed as a dependable B12 strategy.','Children, pregnancy and medically vulnerable adults need individualized professional oversight.'],
        V('Macrobiotic safety scan','checklist',[{label:'Fuel',detail:'Enough total energy, fat and meal frequency?'},{label:'Build',detail:'Regular legumes, soy foods or other acceptable protein anchors?'},{label:'Protect',detail:'Reliable B12, vitamin D, calcium, iron, zinc, iodine and omega-3 sources?'},{label:'Monitor',detail:'Growth, weight trend, symptoms, labs and medical treatment?'}]),
        N('Audit in four verbs','FUEL · BUILD · PROTECT · MONITOR', 'Confirm energy, protein structure, nutrient protection and clinical follow-up.'),
        'A food can be wholesome and still be insufficient as the only food category. Brown rice and vegetables cannot by themselves provide every nutrient required across the life cycle.',
        'If a client is using macrobiotics during cancer treatment or another illness, coordinate with the medical team so restriction does not worsen malnutrition or displace effective care.',
        A('A strict macrobiotic client relies on brown rice, vegetables and seaweed. What is the best next step?',[O('Assume the seaweed covers B12 and iodine safely.',false,'Seaweed is not a reliable B12 source and iodine can be highly variable.'),O('Assess energy, protein anchors, fortified foods/supplements, symptoms and life stage.',true,'This converts the diet label into a safety assessment.'),O('Promise the pattern will treat disease.',false,'Therapeutic claims are not supported.')])
        )
      ]},
      {id:'motives',title:'Understand why people choose vegetarian diets',summary:'Separate values and lived experience from claims that need evidence.',moments:[
        M('vn1-m3','Motivations overlap but are not interchangeable','Ethical, environmental, cultural, religious, economic and health motivations can coexist without requiring the same counselling response.',[
          'A person may avoid animal foods because of animal welfare, environmental impact, faith, family tradition, cost, taste, identity or health goals. These motivations influence what advice feels respectful and sustainable. A client motivated by animal ethics may not view eggs as an acceptable protein swap, while a client motivated mainly by cholesterol reduction may be open to different options.',
          'Values are not laboratory findings, and health associations are not moral judgments. Good nutrition education respects the client’s reasons while testing practical claims separately: Is the pattern adequate? Are the proposed substitutions acceptable? Which recommendations are evidence based, and which are preferences?' 
        ],['Ask why the pattern matters to the learner.','Do not frame animal-food avoidance as morally superior or inferior.','Translate goals into acceptable food choices.'],
        V('Motivation wheel','connections',[{label:'Ethics',detail:'Animal welfare and personal values.'},{label:'Environment',detail:'Resource use and food-system impacts.'},{label:'Health',detail:'Risk reduction or symptom goals.'},{label:'Culture/faith',detail:'Identity, tradition and community practice.'}]),
        N('Four common doors','E-E-H-C', 'Ethics, Environment, Health and Culture may all lead to a vegetarian pattern.'),
        'Beginning vegetarians sometimes lean heavily on cheese, eggs or refined substitutes; this is a transition pattern, not an inevitable stage.',
        'Use motivational interviewing: reflect the person’s reason, then collaboratively identify nutrient-smart choices that fit it.',
        A('A client chooses veganism for animal ethics. What is the best first response?',[O('Recommend eggs because they are convenient.',false,'That ignores the stated ethical boundary.'),O('Ask which plant foods and fortified products fit their values and routine.',true,'This respects the motivation while supporting adequacy.'),O('Debate whether the motivation is scientifically valid.',false,'Values and scientific nutrient claims are different questions.')])
        ),
        M('vn1-m4','History explains language, not today’s adequacy','Historical vegetarian movements provide context, but current recommendations must come from current evidence and the person’s actual diet.',[
          'Vegetarian practices long predate modern Western nutrition science. Religious and philosophical traditions in several regions shaped meat avoidance, while nineteenth-century Western reform movements often linked diet with temperance, spirituality and social change. This history helps explain why vegetarianism can be identity-rich rather than a temporary menu choice.',
          'Historical popularity does not verify a nutrient claim, and a modern prevalence statistic can become outdated quickly. Use history to understand vocabulary and community, then use current dietary guidance, reference intakes and research to assess health questions.'
        ],['Vegetarian traditions have multiple cultural origins.','Nineteenth-century Western movements were only one strand.','Historical claims should not be used as clinical proof.'],
        V('Two evidence lanes','timeline',[{label:'History',detail:'Who adopted the pattern, when and why.'},{label:'Current evidence',detail:'What outcomes and requirements are supported now.'},{label:'Practice',detail:'Apply both without confusing one for the other.'}]),
        N('Context is not proof','PAST ≠ PRESCRIPTION', 'Historical practice can explain meaning without determining current recommendations.'),
        'Exact longevity claims such as “vegetarians live seven to nine years longer” depend on population, methods and confounding and should not be taught as universal facts.',
        'When a client repeats a historical or media claim, validate the interest and then classify it as values, history, association or intervention evidence.',
        A('Which use of history is most appropriate?',[O('Use a nineteenth-century claim as a modern supplement guideline.',false,'Supplement guidance must be current.'),O('Use history to understand identity, then assess nutrition with current evidence.',true,'This keeps context and clinical evidence in their proper roles.'),O('Assume every vegetarian follows the same tradition.',false,'Vegetarianism has diverse origins and motivations.')])
        )
      ]},
      {id:'planet',title:'Connect food choices with environmental systems',summary:'Use directionally sound evidence without false precision.',moments:[
        M('vn1-m5','Food production affects several planetary systems','Environmental impact is multidimensional: greenhouse gases, land, freshwater, biodiversity and pollution can move in different directions.',[
          'Food production contributes to greenhouse-gas emissions and uses land and freshwater. Ruminant meat generally has high greenhouse-gas and land impacts, while legumes and grains are often lower per unit of protein. However, results vary with production method, location, yield, transport, waste and the metric being compared.',
          'A single statement such as “beef uses exactly 100 times more water than wheat” is too context dependent to teach as a timeless universal. Directionally, shifting some intake from high-impact animal foods toward legumes, grains, nuts and seeds can reduce several environmental pressures, but transparent boundaries matter.'
        ],['Environmental comparisons depend on the metric.','Direction can be robust even when one ratio is not.','Food waste and production systems also matter.'],
        V('Impact dashboard','systems',[{label:'Climate',detail:'Greenhouse-gas emissions across the life cycle.'},{label:'Land',detail:'Cropland, pasture and habitat conversion.'},{label:'Water',detail:'Blue, green and grey water measures differ.'},{label:'Biodiversity',detail:'Habitat and pollution pressures.'}]),
        N('Do not reduce the planet to one number','C-L-W-B', 'Check Climate, Land, Water and Biodiversity rather than repeating one dramatic ratio.'),
        'Plant foods are not impact-free, and local does not automatically mean lower carbon; production often matters more than distance.',
        'Frame sustainability as a pattern of practical substitutions, not as a purity test or a source of client shame.',
        A('Why is one fixed beef-to-wheat water ratio unreliable?',[O('Water is irrelevant to food production.',false,'Water use is important, but measurement boundaries vary.'),O('The result changes with production system, place and water metric.',true,'Those factors strongly affect a water-footprint comparison.'),O('All plant foods use more water than animal foods.',false,'That is not supported as a general rule.')])
        ),
        M('vn1-m6','Feed conversion helps explain resource demand','Feeding crops to animals adds a biological conversion step, although not every animal feed input is edible by humans.',[
          'Animals use dietary energy and protein for metabolism, movement and tissue maintenance, so only part of feed becomes edible product. This helps explain why many animal products require more feed-crop land than directly eating plant foods. It is accurate to teach the conversion principle without claiming that every kilogram of feed could have fed people.',
          'Livestock systems also use grasslands, crop residues and by-products that humans may not eat. A balanced explanation recognizes these differences while noting that intensive grain-fed systems can increase demand for feed crops and associated land, fertilizer and water.'
        ],['Biological conversion is never 100 percent efficient.','Feed can include human-edible crops and non-edible by-products.','System type changes the comparison.'],
        V('Resource pathway','flow',[{label:'Direct route',detail:'Crop → person.'},{label:'Conversion route',detail:'Crop/feed → animal metabolism → edible product.'},{label:'Context',detail:'Pasture and by-products alter but do not erase the system question.'}]),
        N('Remember the added step','FEED → FUNCTION → FOOD', 'Animals first use feed for living functions before food reaches people.'),
        'Environmental evidence should inform choices without turning nutrition counselling into a debate about personal virtue.',
        'Ask which substitutions are affordable, culturally suitable and nutritionally equivalent rather than only which food has the lowest footprint.',
        A('Which statement is most accurate?',[O('All livestock feed is suitable for direct human consumption.',false,'Pasture, residues and by-products may not be human edible.'),O('Animal production adds a biological conversion step that can increase resource demand.',true,'Metabolic losses are central to feed-conversion comparisons.'),O('Feed conversion proves every animal system has identical impacts.',false,'Systems differ substantially.')])
        )
      ]},
      {id:'practice',title:'Turn identity into a safe study plan',summary:'Assess adequacy, pattern quality and red flags without stereotyping.',moments:[
        M('vn1-m7','A vegetarian label is the start of assessment','A useful assessment links exclusions to replacements, reliable nutrient sources and the person’s life stage.',[
          'Begin with a usual-day or multi-day food picture, including beverages, fortified products, supplements, cooking fats and foods eaten away from home. Then check energy sufficiency, variety and dependable sources of vitamin B12, iron, zinc, iodine, calcium, vitamin D, protein and essential fats as relevant to the pattern.',
          'Assessment should also include access, cooking capacity, cultural foods, gastrointestinal tolerance, medications, laboratory results when available and life stage. It is unsafe to assume that a diet is adequate because it is plant based, or deficient because it excludes meat.'
        ],['Document both exclusions and replacements.','Check reliable B12 in vegan patterns.','Adapt priorities to life stage and clinical context.'],
        V('Assessment funnel','sequence',[{label:'Pattern',detail:'What is included and excluded?'},{label:'Reliability',detail:'Which foods are fortified or supplemented?'},{label:'Context',detail:'Life stage, access, appetite and health.'},{label:'Action',detail:'Prioritize the highest-impact gap.'}]),
        N('Ask four R questions','REMOVE · REPLACE · RELY · REVIEW', 'What was removed? What replaced it? Which source is reliable? When will you review?'),
        'A supplement list is not a diagnosis, and more is not always safer—especially for iodine, iron, vitamin A and vitamin D.',
        'Keep advice within scope and refer when symptoms, labs, growth, pregnancy or medical conditions require individualized care.',
        A('What is the strongest evidence that a vegan client has a dependable B12 plan?',[O('They eat seaweed weekly.',false,'Seaweed is not a dependable B12 source.'),O('They use a labelled fortified food or supplement consistently.',true,'A known, reliable source can be assessed.'),O('They eat many vegetables.',false,'Unfortified vegetables do not reliably supply B12.')])
        ),
        M('vn1-m8','Current evidence can disagree with an old test','When a source question is outdated, preserve it as a historical item but explain the current best-supported answer and uncertainty.',[
          'Older course questions may contain fixed statistics, unit errors or overly absolute statements. A scientifically responsible study hub keeps the original wording visible while separating source answer, current evidence and correction status. A question with two defensible options should not be scored as if only one were possible.',
          'Use a hierarchy: current government guidance and dietary reference values; current professional position papers; high-quality systematic reviews; then foundational textbook explanations. Date-stamp claims likely to change and use “associated with” when evidence is observational.'
        ],['Preserve source wording for traceability.','Flag rather than force ambiguous answers.','Use current authoritative evidence for changing recommendations.'],
        V('Validation ladder','hierarchy',[{label:'1 · Current standards',detail:'Government guidance and reference intakes.'},{label:'2 · Professional guidance',detail:'Scope-specific current position papers.'},{label:'3 · Synthesized research',detail:'Systematic reviews and guidelines.'},{label:'4 · Older text',detail:'Foundation and historical framing.'}]),
        N('Do not force certainty','VERIFY · FLAG · EXPLAIN', 'Verify strong answers, flag defects, and explain what changed.'),
        'The 2025 Academy position addresses adults and should not be stretched into an all-life-stages guarantee.',
        'Tell learners explicitly when a quiz item is preserved but not safely scoreable. This builds evidence literacy as well as test readiness.',
        A('A source question has two defensible correct options. What should the hub do?',[O('Choose one secretly so the quiz can produce a score.',false,'That creates false certainty.'),O('Mark it ambiguous and explain both options.',true,'This protects accuracy and teaches the reasoning.'),O('Delete the original question without a note.',false,'Preserving the source maintains traceability.')])
        )
      ]}
    ],
    sources:[
      {title:'Health Canada — Considerations for vegetarian diets',url:'https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-guide/explore/dietary-guidelines/applying/considerations-vegetarian-diets.html'},
      {title:'Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics — Vegetarian Dietary Patterns for Adults (2025)',url:'https://www.eatrightpro.org/-/media/files/eatrightpro/practice/academy-positions/vegetarian-dietary-patterns-for-adults.pdf'},
      {title:'National Cancer Institute — Definition of macrobiotic diet',url:'https://www.cancer.gov/publications/dictionaries/cancer-terms/def/macrobiotic-diet'},
      {title:'National Cancer Institute — Nutrition in Cancer Care (macrobiotic evidence review)',url:'https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/side-effects/appetite-loss/nutrition-hp-pdq'},
      {title:'NIH Office of Dietary Supplements — Vitamin B12',url:'https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/VitaminB12-HealthProfessional/'}
    ],
    caseStudy:{title:'From label to useful plan',scenario:'Maya says she is “mostly vegan” for environmental and ethical reasons. She eats cereal with oat beverage, sandwiches, pasta and occasional restaurant cheese, but is unsure whether the beverage is fortified and uses no supplement.',prompts:['Which details must be clarified before judging adequacy?','Which nutrient source requires the most immediate reliability check?','How can environmental goals be supported without using a guilt-based message?'],takeaway:'Respect the motivation, define the actual pattern, and turn exclusions into reliable replacements—starting with vitamin B12 and overall pattern quality.'},
    recap:['Vegetarian is an umbrella term, not a nutrient analysis.','Restriction increases the need for purposeful replacement.','Macrobiotic diets vary, are not necessarily vegan and are not proven disease treatments.','A strict macrobiotic pattern requires an energy, protein, micronutrient and life-stage safety audit.','Motivations guide acceptable recommendations.','Environmental comparisons require more than one metric.','Reliable B12 is essential for vegan patterns.','Outdated or ambiguous questions must be flagged, not forced.'],
    flashcards:[
      {front:'Lacto-ovo vegetarian',back:'Includes dairy and eggs while excluding meat, poultry and fish.',tag:'definitions'},{front:'Lacto vegetarian',back:'Includes dairy but excludes eggs and flesh foods.',tag:'definitions'},{front:'Ovo vegetarian',back:'Includes eggs but excludes dairy and flesh foods.',tag:'definitions'},{front:'Vegan dietary pattern',back:'Excludes animal-derived foods; adequacy still depends on actual choices and reliable nutrient sources.',tag:'definitions'},{front:'Why labels are insufficient',back:'They identify exclusions but not food quality, fortification, supplementation, energy intake or life stage.',tag:'assessment'},{front:'Higher-risk restrictive patterns',back:'Raw, fruitarian and other patterns that sharply narrow energy- and nutrient-dense options.',tag:'risk'},{front:'Macrobiotic diet',back:'A variable philosophy-shaped pattern often centred on whole grains, vegetables, legumes and fermented foods; it may or may not include animal foods.',tag:'macrobiotic'},{front:'Macrobiotic evidence boundary',back:'Nutritious components do not prove philosophical mechanisms or disease-treatment claims; NCI reports no proof it treats or prevents cancer recurrence.',tag:'macrobiotic'},{front:'Macrobiotic safety scan',back:'Fuel, build, protect and monitor: energy/fat, protein anchors, reliable micronutrients and clinical trends.',tag:'macrobiotic'},{front:'Reliable vegan B12',back:'Labelled fortified foods and/or supplements—not seaweed, spirulina or unfortified nutritional yeast.',tag:'B12'},{front:'Four environmental dimensions',back:'Climate, land, water and biodiversity.',tag:'environment'},{front:'Feed conversion',back:'Animals use feed for metabolism before some becomes edible product; the extra step can increase resource demand.',tag:'environment'},{front:'Assessment R sequence',back:'Remove, replace, rely, review.',tag:'practice'},{front:'Historical claim',back:'Useful for context but not sufficient as current clinical evidence.',tag:'evidence'},{front:'Ambiguous quiz item',back:'Preserve it, mark it ambiguous and explain the defensible interpretations.',tag:'evidence'}
    ],
    practice:[
      {tag:'definitions',prompt:'Differentiate vegan and lacto-ovo patterns in one sentence.',answer:'A vegan dietary pattern excludes animal-derived foods, while a lacto-ovo pattern retains dairy and eggs but excludes flesh foods.',rationale:'Precise definitions prevent assumptions.'},{tag:'assessment',prompt:'Name four intake details a “vegan” label does not reveal.',answer:'Examples include energy adequacy, legume intake, fortified-food use, B12 supplementation, calcium sources and reliance on refined foods.',rationale:'Actual intake determines adequacy.'},{tag:'risk',prompt:'Why can fruitarian eating be riskier than a varied vegan pattern?',answer:'It adds major restrictions that can displace energy, protein, fats and several micronutrient sources.',rationale:'Risk rises as options narrow.'},{tag:'macrobiotic',prompt:'Why is “macrobiotic equals vegan” incorrect?',answer:'Macrobiotic is a variable philosophy-shaped family of patterns; some versions include fish or other animal foods and others are nearly or fully vegan.',rationale:'Assessment must identify the version actually followed.'},{tag:'macrobiotic',prompt:'Run the four-part safety scan on a strict grain-and-vegetable macrobiotic menu.',answer:'Check fuel/energy and fat, protein anchors, reliable B12/D/calcium/iron/zinc/iodine/omega-3 sources, and growth/weight/symptoms/labs.',rationale:'Wholesome foods do not guarantee a complete diet.'},{tag:'communication',prompt:'Write one respectful opening question for a vegetarian client.',answer:'“Could you walk me through the foods, beverages, fortified products and supplements you usually include?”',rationale:'It gathers data without stereotyping.'},{tag:'environment',prompt:'Why should you avoid one universal beef-to-wheat water ratio?',answer:'Production system, location, product boundaries and the water metric can change the estimate.',rationale:'Environmental figures require context.'},{tag:'systems',prompt:'Explain feed conversion without overstating it.',answer:'Animals use feed for their own metabolism, so only part becomes edible food; however, some feed is pasture or by-product not directly edible by people.',rationale:'This preserves both mechanism and nuance.'},{tag:'evidence',prompt:'How should a fixed longevity claim be phrased?',answer:'As a population-specific association with stated source and date, not a universal number promised to every vegetarian.',rationale:'Longevity is confounded and context dependent.'},{tag:'B12',prompt:'A client relies on spirulina for B12. What is the next teaching point?',answer:'Spirulina is not a dependable B12 source; identify a consistently used labelled fortified food or supplement.',rationale:'Reliable B12 prevents serious deficiency.'},{tag:'evidence',prompt:'What is the correct response to a quiz item with two defensible answers?',answer:'Mark it ambiguous, explain both interpretations and avoid scoring it as certainly right or wrong.',rationale:'False certainty undermines learning.'},{tag:'teach-back',prompt:'Complete: a module is a topic group; a moment is…',answer:'One focused study step inside the module.',rationale:'The course architecture should be explicit.'}
    ]
  };
})();
